Martha Stewart's celebrated benchmark for lemon cake. Full-fat sour cream gives this tall Bundt cake a velvety, tight crumb that holds its moisture for days. Saturated right after cooling with a tart freshly squeezed lemon juice syrup that soaks deep beneath the golden crust, guaranteeing intense citrus brightness in every bite.

*Adapted from [Martha Stewart](https://www.marthastewart.com/1074762/lemon-bundt-cake).*

## Ingredients

### Lemon Sour Cream Cake Batter
- 1 cup (2 sticks) unsalted butter, room temperature (plus extra for greasing pan)
- 3 cups all-purpose flour, spooned and leveled (plus extra for dusting)
- 2 tablespoons finely grated fresh lemon zest (from 2 large lemons)
- ⅓ cup freshly squeezed lemon juice (from 2 large lemons)
- 1 teaspoon baking soda
- 1 teaspoon fine sea salt
- 2 ½ cups granulated sugar
- 6 large eggs, room temperature
- 1 cup full-fat sour cream, room temperature

### Tart Lemon Soaking Syrup
- ½ cup freshly squeezed lemon juice
- ½ cup granulated sugar

## Instructions

1. **Preheat and Prep the Bundt Pan:** Set an oven rack to the middle position and preheat to 350°F (175°C). Thoroughly grease a 12-cup heavy metal Bundt pan with softened butter, reaching all the ridges, flutes, and the center funnel. Dust the entire interior with flour, then turn the pan upside down over the sink and tap firmly to remove any excess flour.
2. **Whisk the Dry Zest Blend:** In a medium bowl, whisk together the flour, lemon zest, baking soda, and fine salt. Whisking the lemon zest with the flour separates the moist zest shreds and coats them in starch, dispersing natural citrus oils evenly throughout the cake.
3. **Cream the Butter and Sugar:** In the bowl of an electric stand mixer fitted with the paddle attachment, beat the softened butter and granulated sugar together on medium-high speed for a full 4 to 5 minutes. The mixture should become very pale, fluffy, and noticeably increased in volume.
4. **Beat in Eggs and Citrus:** Add the room-temperature eggs one by one, beating thoroughly on medium speed after each addition so the emulsion holds. Pour in the ⅓ cup fresh lemon juice and mix until incorporated.
5. **Alternate Flour and Sour Cream:** Set the mixer to its lowest speed. Add one-third of the dry flour mixture, followed by half of the room-temperature sour cream. Add another third of the flour, the remaining sour cream, and finish with the final third of the flour. Mix only until the last streaks of flour disappear; avoid overmixing to preserve a tender crumb.
6. **Level and Tap:** Scrape the thick, velvety batter into the prepared Bundt pan. Smooth the surface with a flexible silicone spatula. Lift the pan 2 inches above the counter and tap it down firmly onto a folded towel twice to force trapped air bubbles to the surface.
7. **Bake to Golden:** Bake at 350°F for 55 to 60 minutes. If the top appears to be browning too quickly at the 45-minute mark, drape a sheet of foil loosely over the top. The cake is done when the top springs back when lightly pressed and a thin wooden skewer inserted into the thickest section emerges clean.
8. **Rest and Turn Out:** Let the cake cool inside the pan on a wire rack for **30 minutes** (this resting period is critical for the cake to release cleanly from the pan). Place a wire rack over the pan and invert in one motion. Lift the pan away and let the cake cool completely.
9. **Simmer and Soak:** In a small saucepan over low heat, combine ½ cup lemon juice and ½ cup sugar. Stir for 2 to 3 minutes until the sugar dissolves completely into a clear, bright syrup. Use a thin wooden skewer or toothpick to poke holes all over the top and fluted sides of the cooled cake. Slowly brush the warm lemon syrup over the cake with a pastry brush in two or three passes, allowing the cake 10 minutes to drink in the syrup between brushings.

## Notes

- **Room Temperature Dairy:** Ensure eggs, butter, and sour cream are all at room temperature before mixing to prevent the batter from curdling and ensure maximum lift.
- **Storage:** Wrap tightly in plastic wrap once the syrup has fully absorbed. Keeps at room temperature for up to 3 days, actually improving in moistness on day two.
